  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows the 594 dam high centered over the \nmid south into southern Appalachia between an upper trough \nacross the Pacific Northwest and another over Ontario and \nQuebec, yielding yet another hot afternoon across our CWA with 2\npm temperatures in the mid to upper 90s, with heat indices in \nthe upper 90s to the lower 100s. The synoptic scale pattern \nremains mostly the same through the weekend, with the center of \nthe H5 high sliding westward over the region tomorrow and \nSaturday. This will keep high temperatures in the mid 90s to as \nhigh as 100 degrees max and heat indices in the upper 90s to as \nhigh as 105 for Friday and Saturday afternoon. Due to the long \nduration of the heat as well as overnight low temperatures \nremaining elevated, went ahead and upgraded the heat advisory to\nan extreme heat warning for the entire CWA through 8 PM \nSaturday.\n\nBy Sunday and into Monday, the center of the H5 high slides a \nbit further west toward the Plains, with mid level heights \nreducing slightly, especially across northern and NE Missouri \nwhere temperatures and heat indices should be a bit cooler \n(especially on Monday). However, temperatures in the upper 90s \nand max heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s should \ncontinue for areas along and south of roughly a St. Joseph to \nBoonville line. By Tuesday, an upper trough moving across the \nIntermountain West and into the Northern Plains should push the \ncenter of the H5 high and mid/upper ridge axis back over our \nCWA, with the H5 high strengthening back toward 595 dam. This \nshould yield high temperatures in the upper 90s to near 100 \ndegrees, with max heat indices up to 105 degrees. Thus, the \nextreme heat warning has been extended through at least 8 PM \nTuesday for these locations along and south of a St. Joseph to \nBoonville line. It is possible that the extreme heat warning may\nneed to be extended into Wednesday, as well.\n\nIncreased forecast certainty arrives by Thursday as some \nguidance suggests an upper trough moving across the Upper \nMidwest and Great Lakes areas and digging southward into the \nOhio Valley on Thursday, potentially sending a cold front into \nthe CWA from the north sometime Thursday afternoon/evening, \nwhich would bring slightly cooler temperatures and chances for \nshowers/storms. Other guidance keeps this trough and front \nfurther north, with the heat dome remaining in place overhead \ninto Thursday and perhaps even Friday. The NBM interquartile \nrange for MaxT at MCI on Thursday is 87 degrees and 97 degrees, \nrespectively, and 84 and 94 degrees, respectively, on Friday.",
